{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-108900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The first term of an arithmetic series is ${a}_{1}=5,$ the common difference is $d=-2.5,$ and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=-105.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-108900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the number of terms, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d).\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${a}_{1}=5$, $d=-2.5$ and ${S}_{N}=-105$ into the above and solve for $N$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ -105 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 5+(N-1)\\cdot (-2.5)) \\\\ -210 & =N(10-2.5N+2.5) \\\\ -210 & =12.5N-2.5{N}^{2} \\\\ 2.5{N}^{2}-12.5N-210 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-(-12.5)\\pm \\sqrt{(-12.5)^{2}-4\\cdot 2.5\\cdot (-210)}}{2\\cdot 2.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{12.5\\pm 47.5}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=12$ or $N=-7.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=12$ terms." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-108900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a1f551e483c07e63c6d7b434f87110bb9354c33b645a026f26459e15efc8069f"} {"question" "q-108900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-108900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "15"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "18"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-108900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-5486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The first term of an arithmetic series is ${a}_{1}=9$, the common difference is $d=8$, and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=636.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-5486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the number of terms, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d).\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${a}_{1}=9$, $d=8$ and ${S}_{N}=636$ into the above and solve for $N$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 636 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 9+(N-1)\\cdot 8) \\\\ 636 & =\\frac{N}{2}(18+8N-8) \\\\ 636 & =\\frac{N}{2}(8N+10) \\\\ 636 & =N(4N+5) \\\\ 636 & =4{N}^{2}+5N \\\\ 4{N}^{2}+5N-636 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-5\\pm \\sqrt{{5}^{2}-4\\cdot 4\\cdot (-636)}}{2\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\pm \\sqrt{10\\,201}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\pm 101}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=12$ or $N=-\\frac{106}{8}.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=12$ terms." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-5486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a1f551e483c07e63c6d7b434f87110bb9354c33b645a026f26459e15efc8069f"} {"question" "q-5486" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-5486" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "14"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "19"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-5486" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-5491" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The first term of an arithmetic series is ${a}_{1}=1.5$, the common difference is $d=3$, and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=150.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-5491" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first term of an arithmetic series is ${a}_{1}=1.5$, the common difference is $d=3$, and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=150.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series.\n\nTo find the number of terms, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d).\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${a}_{1}=1.5$, $d=3$ and ${S}_{N}=150$ into the above and solve for $N$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 150 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot (1.5)+(N-1)\\cdot 3) \\\\ 150 & =\\frac{N}{2}(3+3N-3) \\\\ 150 & =\\frac{N}{2}(3N) \\\\ 150 & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {N}^{2} \\\\ 100 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because $N$ can't be negative." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-5491" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a1f551e483c07e63c6d7b434f87110bb9354c33b645a026f26459e15efc8069f"} {"question" "q-5491" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-5491" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "11"] ["math" "13"] ["math" "15"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-5491" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-108898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The first term of an arithmetic series is ${a}_{1}=1,$ the common difference is $d=\\frac{1}{2},$ and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=52.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-108898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the number of terms, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d).\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${a}_{1}=1,$ $d=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${S}_{N}=52$ into the above and solve for $N$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 52 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 1+(N-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 104 & =N(2+\\frac{N}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 104 & =\\frac{{N}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}N \\\\ {N}^{2}+3N-208 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-208)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{841}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm 29}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=13$ or $N=-16.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=13$ terms." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-108898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a1f551e483c07e63c6d7b434f87110bb9354c33b645a026f26459e15efc8069f"} {"question" "q-108898" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-108898" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "11"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "13"] ["math" "14"] ["math" "15"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-108898" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "13"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-5490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The first term of an arithmetic series is ${a}_{1}=3$, the common difference is $d=6$, and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=243.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-5490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the number of terms, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d).\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${a}_{1}=3$, $d=6$ and ${S}_{N}=243$ into the above and solve for $N$, as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 243 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 3+(N-1)\\cdot 6) \\\\ 243 & =\\frac{N}{2}(6+6N-6) \\\\ 243 & =\\frac{N}{2}(6N) \\\\ 243 & =3{N}^{2} \\\\ {N}^{2} & =81 \\\\ N & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because $N$ can't be negative." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-5490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a1f551e483c07e63c6d7b434f87110bb9354c33b645a026f26459e15efc8069f"} {"question" "q-5490" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-5490" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-5490" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-108904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n2+2.5+3+…=470\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-108904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\\,\\,\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=470,$ and the first term ${a}_{1}=2.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =2.5-2 \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 470 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(2)+(N-1)(0.5)) \\\\ 940 & =N(4+0.5N-0.5) \\\\ 940 & =N(0.5N+3.5) \\\\ 940 & =0.5{N}^{2}+3.5N \\\\ 0.5{N}^{2}+3.5N-940 & =0 \\\\ {N}^{2}+7N-1\\,880 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-7\\pm \\sqrt{(-7)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1\\,880)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7\\pm \\sqrt{7\\,569}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7\\pm 87}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=40$ or $N=-47.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=40$ terms." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-108904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a1f551e483c07e63c6d7b434f87110bb9354c33b645a026f26459e15efc8069f"} {"question" "q-108904" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-108904" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "33"] ["math" "40"] ["math" "47"] ["math" "52"] ["math" "61"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-108904" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "40"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-67077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n6+18+30+…=216\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-67077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\\,\\,\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=216$, and the first term ${a}_{1}=6.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =18-6 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 216 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 6+(N-1)\\cdot 12) \\\\ 216 & =\\frac{N}{2}(12+12N-12) \\\\ 216 & =\\frac{N}{2}(12N) \\\\ 216 & =6{N}^{2} \\\\ 36 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because $N$can't be negative. Therefore, our series has $N=6$ terms." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-67077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a1f551e483c07e63c6d7b434f87110bb9354c33b645a026f26459e15efc8069f"} {"question" "q-67077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-67077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-67077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-67075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n7+21+35+…=175\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-67075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\\,\\,\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=175$, and the first term ${a}_{1}=7.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =21-7 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 175 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 7+(N-1)\\cdot 14) \\\\ 175 & =\\frac{N}{2}(14+14N-14) \\\\ 175 & =\\frac{N}{2}(14N) \\\\ 175 & =7{N}^{2} \\\\ 25 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because $N$can't be negative. Therefore, our series has $N=5$ terms." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-67075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a1f551e483c07e63c6d7b434f87110bb9354c33b645a026f26459e15efc8069f"} {"question" "q-67075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-67075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "15"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-67075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-108910" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series. \n\n$$\n5.5+6+6.5+...=77.5\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-108910" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=77.5,$ and the first term ${a}_{1}=5.5.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =6-5.5 \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula for ${S}_{N}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 77.5 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(5.5)+(N-1)(0.5)) \\\\ 155 & =N(11+0.5N-0.5) \\\\ 155 & =N(10.5+0.5N) \\\\ 155 & =10.5N+0.5{N}^{2} \\\\ 0.5{N}^{2}+10.5N-155 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-10.5\\pm \\sqrt{(-10.5)^{2}-4\\cdot 0.5\\cdot (-155)}}{2\\cdot 0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10.5\\pm 20.5}{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=10$ or $N=-31.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=10$ terms." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-108910" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a1f551e483c07e63c6d7b434f87110bb9354c33b645a026f26459e15efc8069f"} {"question" "q-108910" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-108910" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "21"] ["math" "31"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043389/state.json" "file_sha256" "9df0caecd4385f699e67f169d19a11e66337bb6fdba2c06ac6fe80a4f3b1caf4"} {"question" "q-108910" "field" "s